SUMMARY:[現場] Kurtis Schaeffer: "Poetry in Tibet: Pushing Boundaries of 
 Buddhist Language and Thought"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/kurtis-schaeffer-poetry-in-tibet-push
 ing-boundaries-of-buddhist-language-and-thought
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/kurtis-schaeffer-poetry-in-ti
 bet-pushing-boundaries-of-buddhist-language-and-thought\n\nAbstract:\n\nTi
 betan writers of kāvya-style poetry exploited the structured flexibility 
 of the medium to praise the heroes and ideals of Buddhist tradition\, to i
 nnovate in both literary form and practices of thought\, and at times even
  to transgress the strictures implicit in other genres of Buddhist literat
 ure. This talk explores the popular genre of single verses intended to ill
 ustrate the poetic features described in Daṇḍin's Mirror of Poetry\, a
 nd suggests that kāvya poetry was an important space in elite literature 
 where pre-modern Tibetan writers could openly push the boundaries of Buddh
 ist language\, thought\, and feeling.\n\n \n\nBio:\n\nKurtis Schaeffer is 
 a student of Buddhist literature in Tibet and the Himalayas. He is the tra
 nslator of The Life of the Buddha (2014) and Buddhist Meditation: Classic 
 Teachings from Tibet (2024)\, both from Penguin Classics\, and the author 
 of Himalayan Hermitess (2004)\, The Culture of the Book in Tibet (2009)\, 
 and other books. He is the Frances Myers Ball Professor in the Department 
 of Religious Studies at the University of Virginia.
